Subject: [PATCH] mail: Add just archive
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Change-Id: nrwuukvrvtstqmlnkqyzwvynzvstkxzq

Archiving a message means moving its file under maildir/archive/, and
doing that by hand with notmuch search and mv is slow enough that mail
piles up in the inbox. A tag is quick to set from notmuch-emacs, but it
lives only in the local database: it is lost when the database is
rebuilt and nobody else sees it.

archive turns the tag into the file move. It finds every message tagged
archive that still has a file in maildir/cur or maildir/new, moves that
file into the matching directory under maildir/archive/, unchanged and
under the same name, and reindexes so the database follows the move.
Files already archived, and files elsewhere such as drafts, are left
alone, so it is safe to run again. The move is left in the working copy
to be committed like any other change to the list.
---
This is against the mail branch; base-commit is mail@upstream.
Like "[PATCH] mail: Add just commit-mail" [2] it appends to the
Justfile, so whichever lands second needs a trivial rebase.

Archive layout follows "[PATCH 0/3] Archive messages that need no
answer" [1]: files move unchanged under maildir/archive/. That series
is not needed to apply this one, but with it the moved messages get
the derived "archived" tag and lose "inbox" on the reindex. Until
then the move works and the messages simply keep their tags.

The "archive" tag is only the request; "archived" is what the tree
says. A message in new/ goes to archive/new/ rather than cur/, so the
move never changes a file's maildir state.

On the emacs side, setting notmuch-archive-tags to '("+archive") makes
the stock "a" key do the tagging.

Tested in a scratch maildir with its own NOTMUCH_CONFIG: tagged files
in cur/ and new/ moved, untagged ones stayed, the tag followed the
move, and a second run changed nothing.

